 3. Exclusive: Texas AG hopeful&#x27;s &quot;AI audit&quot; plan 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Johnson speaks at a press conference at the Texas Capitol in August 2025. Photo: Mikala Compton/Austin American-Statesman via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nathan Johnson, the Democratic nominee for Texas attorney general, is pitching a plan to examine how existing state laws can be updated to protect constituents from AI harms.

 Why it matters: State attorneys general have largely focused on wielding existing law to bring lawsuits against tech companies.

 Johnson wants to go a step further and get the legislature to pass laws that account for the AI age. Driving the news: Johnson wants to look at consumer protection, fair competition and employment laws and come up with a specific list of recommendations for the legislature — all within the first 30 days in office, according to an announcement shared first with Axios.

 "Stuff that we thought the statutes were fine to handle three years ago might as well have been 3,000 years ago. The pace is changing so rapidly," Johnson told Axios. Texas has passed AI-specific laws, but Johnson says they still fall short — for example, by not requiring employers to disclose when they use AI to determine who gets fired. The big picture: The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has been gutted during President Trump's second term. It has lost much of its workforce and the agency has dropped dozens of investigations and transparency practices , prompting Democratic and Republican attorneys general to take on more consumer protection work.

 States have also been moving forward on AI regulation, while Washington has focused on a deregulatory approach coupled with preempting state action. Full story 

 
 
